<?xml version="1.0"?>
<!--
  arm: 6-axis desktop arm - base yaw, shoulder pitch (21:1 cycloidal drive), elbow pitch (GT2 belt),
  FOREARM ROLL (GT2 belt in the roll frame at the elbow, cad/assemblies/forearm_roll_drive.py), wrist pitch (GT2
  belt) and WRIST ROLL (GT2 belt from the NEMA17 pancake on the wrist_pitch axis, cad/assemblies/wrist_roll_drive.py)
  - + MG996R parallel gripper. Six joints are driven by
  NEMA 17 + MKS SERVO42D kits: the base_yaw / elbow_pitch / wrist_pitch motors are NEMA 17s on the
  mounts the links carry (cad/lib/mounts.py: the 48 mm nema17_48mm#1 under the base, nema17_40mm#2..3 on
  the links, + mks_servo42d#1..3, in base_link / upper_arm_link / forearm_link), the shoulder_pitch one
  the cycloidal drive's own 48 mm motor + board, the forearm_roll one the roll drive's own 40 mm motor
  + board (in elbow_link), the wrist_roll one the wrist roll drive's pancake + board (in wrist_pitch_link);
  which CAN id (software/control/src/config.py J1..J3 - three of the six) drives which joint is unconfirmed.

  SOURCE OF TRUTH: this file. Every number is derived from the CAD by cad/tools/robot/derive.py
  (cad/robot/frames.py = link partition + joint frames; cad/reference/placements.json = the
  SolidWorks capture) and verified with `./cadtool python tools/robot/derive.py`, check mode
  (tests/test_robot.py runs it). Regenerate meshes with cad/tools/robot/export_link_meshes.py.

  FRAMES (REP-103): base_link on the base's bottom face at the base_yaw axis, Z up, X forward
  (the direction the arm extends). Every joint frame has Z on the joint axis and X along the
  child link's long direction at the capture pose. A child link's frame IS its joint frame at
  the SolidWorks capture pose: ALL JOINTS ARE 0 AT THE CAPTURE POSE and every mesh origin is
  identity. In that pose shoulder_link is yawed +3.694 deg about base_yaw relative to base X (a
  captured pose, not a design offset); the shoulder_pitch, elbow_pitch and wrist_pitch axes are
  parallel (SlabParams.elbow_x and -WebParams.wrist_x apart in the pitch plane, offset along the axis as the
  joint origins below put them: cad/robot/frames.py). The capture pose is the design's: the forearm and
  everything beyond it are turned half a turn about the forearm_roll axis from the SolidWorks capture
  (cad/lib/placements.py TURNS: the wrist_pitch motor on the upper arm's side), so at zero the wrist and the
  gripper are rolled 180 deg from the capture and wrist_pitch's axis is the capture's -N.
  forearm_roll's Z runs along the forearm (the pitch plane's in-plane direction) through the WRIST
  CENTRE - on the wrist_pitch axis 17 mm back from the wrist_pitch origin, where the SolidWorks
  wrist_roll axis met it; its X is the pitch-axis direction (its child's long direction IS the axis).
  wrist_roll no longer meets it: the wrist roll drive's pancake sits on the wrist_pitch axis and belts
  to the roll axis WRIST_ROLL_OFFSET across it, WRIST_ROLL_AXIS_H over the wrist plate - a wrist
  offset, the last three axes are NOT concurrent (an inverse kinematics must be numeric). It does NOT cross the elbow axis: the
  roll motor sits on the elbow axis and the roll housing ELBOW_ROLL_OFFSET (the drive's centre
  distance: the stock belt round its tensioner) above it, so forearm_roll's origin sits that far across elbow_link's frame (its -Y) - an elbow
  offset. elbow_link is the elbow's driven side (the 90T pulley carrying the roll drive's stator, the
  frame whose web is the elbow coupler), forearm_link the roll drive's rotor (the pulley + the shaft) + the forearm.

  UNITS: metres, kilograms, radians. MESHES: meshes/<link>.stl in millimetres (scale 0.001),
  one per link in the link frame (visual and collision reuse them; simplified collision
  primitives are a TODO). INERTIALS: OCP BRepGProp on the CAD solids, printed parts at PETG
  1.27 g/cm3, purchased parts at their datasheet/estimated mass (cad/lib/params.py).

  PLACEHOLDERS [ESTIMATE] (cad/lib/params.py): the base_yaw limit and the shoulder's lower limit, every
  effort and velocity value, the jaw travel range; the positive-motion sign of every axis
  (cad/robot/frames.py; confirm with viewer sweeps). The other limits are [DESIGN]
  (cad/tests/test_sweeps.py, the printed hard stops).
  ASSUMPTIONS: j1_coupler (the holder) turns on the base and carries the cycloidal drive's
  stator (the held carrier - motor plate, output hub + pins -, the motor, the gear train) in its
  yoke - together shoulder_link; the drive's rotor (the turning shell: shell ring, ring pins,
  housing bolts + nuts, both 6814s) is bolted to j1_link - together upper_arm_link - so the drive IS the
  shoulder_pitch joint (cad/docs/cycloidal_drive.md, cad/assemblies/cycloidal_drive.py BODIES);
  the GT2 90T pulleys + the wrist's J3 coupler are bolted to the driven links (elbow_link at elbow_pitch,
  wrist_pitch_link at wrist_pitch; the base_yaw 120T to j1_coupler's stub end inside the base - shoulder_link); the wrist
  roll drive's stator (the pancake + its board, its 20T, the belt, its 6806 pair) rides with wrist_link (its frame) in
  wrist_pitch_link, its rotor (pulley + shaft) with the gripper in wrist_roll_link; each belt joint's 6806-2RS bearing pair rides with its housing
  (base_link, upper_arm_link, forearm_link; cad/lib/mounts.py), the base_yaw thrust bearing's lower washer and needle
  cage with base_link, its upper washer with j1_coupler (shoulder_link); the MG996R crank linkage is merged into wrist_roll_link;
  jaw_b mirrors jaw_a (mimic, multiplier -1); positive jaw_a = opening.
  UNCONFIRMED: which CAN id (software/control/src/config.py J1..J3) drives which joint - the motors are placed
  (cad/lib/mounts.py) but not mapped; the shoulder_pitch motor is the drive's own NEMA 17 at 21:1
  (cad/lib/params.py CYCLOIDAL_RATIO; software/control/src/config.py still carries gear_ratio 1.0 everywhere). The
  belt motors' masses (280 g) and boards (35 g) are estimates; the wrist_pitch motor sits where the stock
  wrist belt puts it (J2_MOTOR_SLIDE_X, WRIST_BELT_LENGTH); FOREARM_ROLL_LIMIT_DEG stops 2 deg short of the
  printed hard stop, WRIST_ROLL_LIMIT_DEG 2 deg short of the wrist roll's. The jaw travel (JAW_TRAVEL_MM) is an
  estimate that does not fit the gripper (cad/docs/open_issues.md).
  The jaws are not driven by software/control/src/config.py, and forearm_roll and wrist_roll need CAN ids of
  their own (config.py names fewer than the arm's boards).
-->
